{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development would result in the loss of a family-sized dwellinghouse, the need for which is clearly demonstrated in the development plan and supporting evidence base. While it would provide a dwelling in the form of an HMO, it fails to demonstrate that this meets an identified need, and further would not provide an adequate quality of accommodation to support a high standard of living, would cause a loss of local amenity including to neighbouring occupiers, and would not be located in an area with a high PTAL rating. As such, the proposal is considered to have a detrimental impact on the borough&#39;s housing supply and is contrary to the following policies:&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)Policies&nbsp;D6, D14 and T4&nbsp;of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP3, SP8, DMD1, DMT1, DMH5 and DMSI3&nbsp;of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan&nbsp;(September 2024)",
    "By reason of bulk, mass, scale and siting, the proposed&nbsp;two storey front extension would disproportionately increase the size of the modest-sized dwelling,&nbsp;unbalancing the uniformity of the terrace row and resulting in the reduced presence of the key bookend feature, thus creating a discordant and fragmented appearance. The proposal would impinge upon the openness of the corner plot therefore posing harm to the appearance of the streetscape. The development is considered to be at odds with the character of the dwelling and&nbsp;the meticulously considered and planned estate. As such, the proposal is considered contrary to:Paragraphs 135 and 139 of the&nbsp;&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (December 2023)Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP2 and DMD1 of the&nbsp;Barking and Dagenham Local Plan&nbsp;(September 2024)",
    "The proposed HMO has the potential to increase activity level and coming and goings to and from the site leading to additional levels of noise and disturbance at the property. This is considered to have a negative impact on neighbouring amenity in this street of family housing and in turn the health and wellbeing of neighbouring residents. As such the proposal is contrary to the following:Policies DMD 1, SP 7 and DMSI 3 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan&nbsp; (September 2024)",
    "The proposed development fails to demonstrate that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies which seek to ensure proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision: National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2023) Policies T1 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021) Policy DMT2 of the Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
